<p><strong>A salon or spa wins customers back by noticing who has not returned, contacting them at the right moment and asking happy customers for reviews. By hand this rarely happens, so it works best when it is automatic.</strong></p>
<p><em>The scene below is a typical one, not a single named customer.</em></p>
<h2>The regular nobody noticed leaving</h2>
<p>A customer used to come every month. Then three months pass, then six. Nobody at the front desk notices, because there is no list of who is overdue. She has quietly moved to another salon, and you never had the chance to ask why.</p>
<h2>Why it happens</h2>
<ul><li>Visit history is spread across notebooks, phones and memory.</li><li>Following up is nobody's daily job.</li><li>New customers get all the attention, so existing ones drift.</li></ul>
<h2>How it is solved</h2>
<p>Zennly's <a href="/features/outreach/">customer outreach</a> sends win-back messages and review requests automatically by email, with an <strong>approval queue</strong> so you check what goes out. SMS and WhatsApp are available on request. Add a dated offer from <a href="/features/campaigns/">promotions</a> if you want to give a reason to return.</p>
<h2>Reviews, too</h2>
<p>The same flow asks happy customers for a review after a visit. Reviews on Google, G2 and similar sites are also what search engines and AI assistants read when someone asks for a recommendation.</p>
<h2>A quick fix you can start today</h2>
<ol><li>List customers who have not visited in 60 to 90 days.</li><li>Send a short, personal message with one clear reason to come back.</li><li>Ask your five happiest customers this week for a Google review.</li></ol>
